# Git Branch Management Notes (MacBook)

## 1. Clone a repository

Clone the repository only once:

```bash
git clone https://git.1digitalstack.com/ods-poc/admin-console.git
```

Enter the project folder:

```bash
cd admin-console
```

> Always run Git commands from inside the project folder, not from Desktop.

---

## 2. Check local branches

Show only branches that exist locally:

```bash
git branch
```

Example:

```bash
* main
```

---

## 3. Fetch a specific remote branch

When you want to bring a branch from GitLab to your local machine, without fetching every branch:

```bash
git fetch origin dev:refs/remotes/origin/dev
```

This fetches only the `dev` branch and updates the remote-tracking reference `origin/dev`.

---

## 4. Create a local branch from the remote branch

After fetching, create a local branch that tracks the remote branch:

```bash
git switch --track origin/dev
```

Now your local `dev` branch is connected to `origin/dev`.

Verify:

```bash
git branch -vv
```

---

## 5. Pull the latest changes

If you are already on the `dev` branch:

```bash
git pull
```

Or explicitly:

```bash
git pull origin dev
```

---

## 6. Bring another branch when needed

For example, when you need `staging` in the future:

### Fetch the branch

```bash
git fetch origin staging:refs/remotes/origin/staging
```

### Create and switch to the local branch

```bash
git switch --track origin/staging
```

Repeat this process for any branch you need.

---

## 7. Useful Git commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `git branch` | Show local branches |
| `git branch -r` | Show remote-tracking branches |
| `git branch -vv` | Show local branches and tracking information |
| `git status` | Check working tree status |
| `git fetch origin dev:refs/remotes/origin/dev` | Fetch only the dev branch |
| `git switch dev` | Switch to an existing local branch |
| `git pull` | Update current branch |
| `git switch main` | Switch to main |
| `git branch -d dev` | Delete local dev branch safely |
| `git branch -D dev` | Force delete local dev branch |

---

## 8. Recommended workflow

### First time bringing a branch from GitLab

```bash
git fetch origin dev:refs/remotes/origin/dev
git switch --track origin/dev
```

### Next time, update the same branch

```bash
git switch dev
git pull
```

### When you need a new branch in the future

```bash
git fetch origin staging:refs/remotes/origin/staging
git switch --track origin/staging
```

---

## Important

- `git clone` — downloads the repository initially.
- `git fetch` — downloads updates/branch information without changing your working files.
- `git switch --track` — creates a local branch connected to the remote branch.
- `git pull` — fetches and integrates updates into your current branch.
- You don't need to create all remote branches locally.
- Keep only the branches you actually need for your work.
- Avoid using `git branch -D` unless you are sure you don't need the local branch or its work.
